#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101837} :activity/id #uuid "6f288c2e-8f4d-5306-9c16-f6582046db0f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049040 :topic/id #uuid "d56ad601-79c5-5201-9340-f03bd4da4f1f" :topic/math-academy-id 1262} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101931 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x)$ for the piecewise function $f(x)$ given below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ 2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=1 \\\\ -(x-1)^{3}+3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by sketching a graph of the function:\n\n![](images/01/01e1704152432a01a1491f6d7575ada56907272e53f5eeee8a90b8bc96f3eb51.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x) = x^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-(x-1)^3+3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}-(x-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =-(1-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist: \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x) = \\text{DNE}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "80db4900-d9c5-5900-b975-5c26b8f713e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 236 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d1483e1b-2de0-5e5b-a8d1-f4d81897891d" :step/math-academy-id 424 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100977 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100753 :question/id #uuid "62d73be9-5f67-5f44-a45a-f5c25a4dfe27" :question/math-academy-id "e-64"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050454}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362398}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Coincide"} :step/id #uuid "51e2792d-7ab3-57f6-a8aa-4475d65fedf7" :step/math-academy-id 425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100978 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100754 :question/id #uuid "e9a033d1-eb3f-5e7e-8237-346b03681499" :question/math-academy-id "e-65"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392101}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"} :step/id #uuid "e1dad1df-a9ec-5939-84eb-1443d3e7f165" :step/math-academy-id 428 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101932 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)$ for the piecewise function given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an idea of the behavior of the function around $x=0,$ we can plot the function.\n\n![](images/78/78a832c65a4bb5862694717f52704a61660bd81a34eb7a72cfeeb98a52bb50f1.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3^{x+1}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{3}^{x+1} \\\\  & ={3}^{0+1} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt {x^2+9}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe left and right-sided limits both exist and are equal. So, the overall limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x) =3.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "416b850c-1c8d-5794-a787-3a59d48d0616" :tutorial/math-academy-id 239 :tutorial/title "Limits of Piecewise Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f2bafd4a-88b6-500a-b45d-6de37cdf6804" :step/math-academy-id 430 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100979 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100758 :question/id #uuid "7f534444-765b-5c3a-a381-883a48911700" :question/math-academy-id "e-66"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402495}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"} :step/id #uuid "9a5952ff-0a94-5306-8005-3e5b53635c3